Face Match Pipeline Using Dlib:
This pipeline verifies the student's identity in real time during an online exam using Dlib's HOG-based frontal face detector for face detection and Dlib's ResNet-based face recognition model for identity matching. 
This approach offers good accuracy, is efficient on CPU, and performs well even under variable lighting conditions. All identity checks, including timestamps, match results, and face crops (if mismatched), are logged in a secure database for audit.
i. Webcam Frame Capture
Frames are continuously captured from the student's webcam for identity verification throughout the exam. 
To improve processing speed,  every frame is passed through face detection, while cached results are used for intermediate frames.
ii. Face Detection Using Dlib HOG Detector
Every sampled frame is first enhanced and applied in the LAB color space to normalize lighting conditions. 
The enhanced frame is then passed through Dlib's HOG-based frontal face detector with one upsample pass to improve detection of smaller or more distant faces.
If no face is detected, the absence is logged with a timestamp and monitoring continues. If one or more faces are detected, the bounding box coordinates are scaled back to the original frame resolution for further processing.
iii. Multiple Person Detection
The pipeline checks the number of detected faces in the frame. If more than one face is detected, the event is treated as a multi-person violation. A timestamped frame with an on-screen warning overlay is saved, the violation is logged with a warning number, the event is stored in the database, and a critical alert is raised if the multi-person warning count reaches the predefined threshold.
iv. Face Embedding Generation (Dlib ResNet)
When exactly one face is detected and a periodic identity check is due (every 5 seconds), the detected face region is passed through Dlib's 68-point shape predictor to obtain facial landmarks. These landmarks are then used by Dlib's ResNet-based face recognition model to compute a 128-dimensional face embedding that encodes the person's identity. The same procedure is applied to the pre-registered reference image at enrollment time.
v. Face Matching and Verification
The Euclidean distance between the live face embedding and the registered embedding is computed. If the distance is less than or equal to 0.6 (the match threshold), the identity is verified successfully and monitoring continues. If the distance exceeds 0.6, the system logs the mismatch event with timestamp and distance, saves the cropped face image for review, raises a warning, and triggers a critical alert if the mismatch warning count reaches the defined threshold.
vi. Continuous or Periodic Identity Check
Face matching is performed periodically every 5 seconds rather than on every frame, balancing monitoring reliability with computational efficiency. Face detection for multi-person checks, however, runs continuously on every sampled frame.
vii. Logging and Audit Trail
All checks including timestamp, match status (match or mismatch), Euclidean distance, number of faces detected, and cropped face image path (for mismatches) are recorded in the database. This enables reliable post-exam review and strengthens academic integrity.



